# 2. Uniform positivity

Let

\[
\Sigma=\{P_a:a\in A\}
\]

be a finite family of \(n\times n\) row-stochastic matrices.

Assume

\[
\boxed{
P_a(i,j)>0
\quad
\forall a,i,j.
}
\]

Because the family is finite,

\[
\eta
=
\min_{a,i,j}
P_a(i,j)
>0.
\]

For a stochastic matrix \(P\), Dobrushin's coefficient is

\[
\delta(P)
=
\frac12
\max_{i,k}
\sum_j|P_{ij}-P_{kj}|.
\]

Equivalent formula:

\[
\delta(P)
=
1-
\min_{i,k}
\sum_j
\min(P_{ij},P_{kj}).
\]

Since every entry is at least \(\eta\),

\[
\sum_j\min(P_{ij},P_{kj})
\ge
n\eta.
\]

Therefore every generator satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\delta(P_a)
\le
1-n\eta
=:
\rho
<1.
}
\]

---

# 3. Universal pair synchronization

For arbitrary probability distributions \(p,q\),

\[
TV(pP,qP)
\le
\delta(P)TV(p,q).
\]

For a word

\[
w=a_1\cdots a_k,
\]

submultiplicativity gives

\[
TV(pP_w,qP_w)
\le
\prod_{t=1}^{k}
\delta(P_{a_t})
TV(p,q)
\le
\rho^kTV(p,q).
\]

Hence:

### Theorem CW-002F.1 — Strict positivity forces uniform synchronization

For every pair \(p,q\),

\[
\boxed{
\inf_w
TV(pP_w,qP_w)
=
0.
}
\]

Indeed every sufficiently long word works; the controller does not need to optimize the letters.

More strongly, every infinite switching sequence forgets its initial distribution exponentially fast.

