# 6. PFA value-1 collapses under uniform positivity

Consider a probabilistic automaton whose transition matrices all have entries at least \(\eta>0\).

If the accepting set \(F\) is a proper subset of the state set and at least one letter is read, then every output state has probability at least \(\eta\).

Hence for every nonempty word,

\[
\operatorname{Acc}(w)
\le
1-\eta |F^c|
<1.
\]

Therefore the value cannot approach one unless the acceptance convention has a trivial all-accepting escape or empty-word special case.

This explains structurally why the value-1 reduction in CW-002E necessarily relies on zeros/support restrictions.

The undecidability mechanism needs probability mass to become arbitrarily concentrated through forbidden transitions or increasingly selective paths.

Uniform positivity destroys that mechanism by maintaining a fixed rejection floor.

This is closely aligned with Rabin's 1963 observation that strictly positive "actual automata" have much stronger stability properties than general probabilistic automata.

